Make an empty address regex stand for the last regex used.
POSIX and GNU sed define an empty regex, as in "//p", as the last regex
used when the command runs, and sed does that for s//. But an empty
address regex was bound when the script was parsed, to the last address
regex before it in the script, ignoring s commands. So
"sed -n 's/a/X/;//p'" died with "no previous regexp" instead of
printing the changed lines, and in "/x/p;s/y/Z/;//d" the // meant /x/
instead of /y/. The parser also left its last address regex as the
initial previous regex at run time, so an s// that ran before any regex
silently used one that came later in the script. Mark an empty address
regex instead, and resolve it when the address is tested. Record each
address regex as the last one used whenever it is tested, matching or
not, as is done for s and as GNU sed does, so that within a range the
end regex is the last one used once it has been tried.

---
 editors/sed.c |   37 ++++++++++++++++++++-----------------
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -178,6 +178,9 @@
